UNIT directive
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@6199e2cc8b75 cli/sir/v05.mjs export directive — the parser's answer for one directive: the first directive named name in sir_parse's legacy-mode parse (cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs parse + get), its arguments and non-empty continuation pieces joined; reproduce it exactly, accidents included
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG directive(text: text, name: text) -> text | none
ENVELOPE text is any well-formed text (every kind of line break, byte-order marks, non-ASCII and astral characters, quotes
  and backslashes allowed). name is any text; a name outside the NAME grammar, and the name "CITED", are IN scope as throw observations (R1).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Read one directive's value out of a SIR file the way the SIR parser reads it: find the FIRST directive line
  whose name is name and return its arguments followed by every non-empty continuation piece that belongs to it, separated
  by single spaces; comments and attribute groups are not part of the value, and a "#" or an attribute keyword inside a
  quoted span is. A bare directive yields the empty text; an absent directive yields none. Only SPACE and CHARACTER TABULATION (the SEP set) ever separate, trim, blank a line or
  border a comment marker; every other character, any other whitespace included, is content. The rules restate exactly the
  parser behavior the value depends on.
BEHAVIOR
R1 NAME. name must match the NAME grammar, else -> throws, before text is read at all. This includes the empty text, a
  lowercase name, "SIG:", a name with a space, a dot or a trailing line break, and non-ASCII letters. The name "CITED" (the
  attribute-only keyword, which never starts a directive, R6) also -> throws, whatever text holds.
R2 LINES. text is cut into physical lines at each CARRIAGE RETURN + LINE FEED pair (one break), each lone CARRIAGE
  RETURN and each lone LINE FEED. LINE SEPARATOR, PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR and NEXT LINE do NOT break a line. Each line then
  loses every BYTE ORDER MARK inside its LEAD — the longest leading run of SEP characters and BYTE ORDER MARKs, in any mix
  (the SEP characters stay, in order) — and after that every SEP character at its very end. One pass reaches the fixed
  point: BOM SPACE BOM "x" becomes SPACE "x" (so an unindented line can become indented). A mark after the first character
  outside the LEAD is content. CITED FIDELITY package
R3 SKIPPED lines. A line left empty by R2 is skipped (a line of NO-BREAK SPACE, LINE TABULATION or any other non-SEP
  character is NOT empty). Otherwise its INDENT is its leading run of SEP characters (possibly empty) and its CONTENT is
  the rest. A line whose CONTENT starts with "#" is a comment and is skipped. Skipped lines never end a directive: lines
  after them still continue it.
R4 QUOTED SPANS. CONTENT is scanned left to right for quoted spans. A QUOTATION MARK OPENS a span only at a token start: it
  is the first character of CONTENT, or the character before it is in the OPENER set, or it is the character directly after
  the closing mark of the span just closed (two literals back to back). From the opener the span runs to and
  including the next QUOTATION MARK that is not the partner of an escape pair, where an escape pair is a REVERSE SOLIDUS plus
  the ONE character after it, whatever that character is (QUOTATION MARK, REVERSE SOLIDUS, LINE SEPARATOR or an astral
  character alike). The scan resumes after the closing mark. When CONTENT ends first (no closing mark, or a REVERSE SOLIDUS
  last), that opener protects nothing and no later span exists on the line. Any other QUOTATION MARK — an inch mark after a digit, one
  after a letter, a SOLIDUS, "*", "+" or "?", a closing parenthesis or bracket, a QUOTATION MARK that closed no span, a NO-BREAK SPACE or a
  byte-order mark — opens nothing; a closing mark need not end a token. CITED FIDELITY package
R5 COMMENT CUT. On every non-skipped line, CONTENT is cut at the FIRST "#" that has a SEP character immediately before it,
  a SEP character or the end of CONTENT immediately after it, and does not lie strictly inside a quoted span (R4): that
  "#", the SEP run immediately before it and everything after it are dropped. The result is CODE. "a#b", "a #b", a "#"
  next to a NO-BREAK SPACE or IDEOGRAPHIC SPACE, and a "#" inside a quoted span are not cuts.
R6 DIRECTIVE LINE. A line with an empty INDENT is examined as a candidate directive: its NAME-WORD is CODE up to (not
  including) the first SEP character, or all of CODE when there is none; REST is CODE from that SEP character on (it keeps
  its leading separator), or the empty text when there is none. A NAME-WORD that is a NAME followed by exactly one ":" is
  read as that NAME ("SIG: x" is SIG, "SIG:: x" is not). When the NAME-WORD (so read) matches the NAME grammar and is not
  the attribute keyword "CITED", the line STARTS a new directive with that name — whether or not the name is a known
  directive; "EARNED" and "ANCHOR" do start directives — and its first piece is ATTR-CUT(REST). Any other character after the
  name ("SIGNAL", "SIG-X", NO-BREAK SPACE, IDEOGRAPHIC SPACE, LINE SEPARATOR) makes it a different NAME-WORD: "ORACLE" never
  matches an "ORACLE-CLASS" line, and a name glued to its arguments by non-SEP whitespace or by an invisible format character
  (ZERO WIDTH SPACE, WORD JOINER, MONGOLIAN VOWEL SEPARATOR) is not a directive; nor is "NAME:" glued that way, nor a line
  whose CONTENT starts with such a character before a name (the parser reports these; the value ignores the report).
R7 CONTINUATION. Every other non-skipped line — an indented line, or an unindented line whose NAME-WORD fails R6 (prose,
  lowercase words, a line beginning with NO-BREAK SPACE, a column-0 "CITED" or "CITED:" line) — continues the most recent
  directive started above it, contributing the piece ATTR-CUT(SPACE + CODE). Before any directive has started such a line
  is ignored.
R8 ATTR-CUT(s). An ATTRIBUTE KEYWORD is one of the words CITED/EARNED/ANCHOR occurring as a whole token: preceded by one
  or more SEP characters, followed by a SEP character or the end of s, and not lying inside a quoted span of the line's
  CONTENT (R4), where it is data. ATTR-CUT keeps the part of s before the first such run-and-keyword, with leading and
  trailing SEP characters removed; with no keyword it is s with leading and trailing SEP characters removed. Since both a directive line's REST and a continuation's piece begin with a separator, a keyword
  FIRST on either kind of line yields the empty piece; a keyword glued to other letters, or followed by any non-SEP
  character (":" or ","), is content.
R9 RESULT. The first directive in text order whose name equals name (exactly, case-sensitively) decides: its pieces (first
  the directive line's, then each continuation's, in order) are filtered to the NON-EMPTY ones and joined with one SPACE
  between neighbours; nothing else is trimmed. No non-empty piece -> "" (the empty text). No directive named name -> none.
  Later directives of the same name never count.
PINNED grammar NAME = an ASCII capital letter A-Z followed by zero or more of A-Z, 0-9 and "-" CITED R1, R6 — the parser's directive-name grammar; it subsumes the R-number form ("R" then digits)
PINNED charset OPENER = { SPACE, CHARACTER TABULATION, "(", "[", "{", ",", "=", ":", ";", "|", "<", ">", "!", "&", "-", "~" } CITED R4 — the sixteen characters after which a QUOTATION MARK starts a token; nothing else: not "*", "+" or "?" (regex quantifiers), no other whitespace, no SOLIDUS, letter, digit or closing bracket
PINNED charset SEP = { SPACE, CHARACTER TABULATION } CITED R2, R3, R5, R6, R8 — the one whitespace set; NO-BREAK SPACE, LINE TABULATION, FORM FEED, IDEOGRAPHIC SPACE, LINE SEPARATOR, NEXT LINE and the byte-order mark (except those R2 drops) are content
INVARIANT a non-none result never begins or ends with a SEP character, never contains LINE FEED or CARRIAGE RETURN, and is empty only when every piece was empty.
ORACLE value -> oracles/directive.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 28f48ce2c793b0d474c21f1581e4871f1c4832cd4791a26de556fa05f6530554
SCOPE IN: R1–R9 — the value of one directive as sir_parse's legacy-mode parse reads it. OUT: parse diagnostics (errors,
  warnings incl. the name-separator, glued-colon and invisible-lead reports, strict mode), attribute values, comment text, indentation statistics, the
  directive vocabulary's registers (an unknown but grammatical name is still a directive here), and non-text arguments.
PROVENANCE rederive@6199e2cc8b75 (rederive, the SIR toolchain), cli/sir/v05.mjs export directive over cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs
  parse + get; pinned authority snapshot selfhost/.authority/6199e2cc8b75, driven through selfhost/seams/directive.mjs (an
  absent directive is none). Round 3: re-cut for the #43 parser (one SEP whitespace rule, keyword-first attribute split,
  empty pieces dropped, a column-0 "CITED" line a continuation, quoted spans protect "#"). Round 4: re-cut for the 978444f parser (quoted spans open only at a token start, an escape
  pair takes any one character, a quoted attribute keyword is data, the name "CITED" throws). Round 5: re-cut for the bb8ded8 parser (#75 the
  nineteen-character OPENER set and back-to-back literals, #76 a byte-order mark after the indent dropped, #83 glued "NAME:"
  and invisible leads reported, not directives). Round 5b: re-cut for the 6199e2c parser (#99 every byte-order mark in the
  leading SEP-and-mark run dropped in one pass, #107 "*", "+" and "?" no longer OPENERs).
